WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.955
A vector in three dimensions records three directed components

00:00:03.955 --> 00:00:09.880
For one, two, two, the length comes from adding the squares of all three components

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:16.200
These bars display the squared contributions, whose sum is nine and whose square root is three.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.380
Addition combines corresponding coordinates

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.081
Add the first components, then the second, then the third

00:00:24.081 --> 00:00:35.833
The drawn arrows show the horizontal projection, while the formula retains the height component; a two-dimensional picture cannot show every coordinate of the three-dimensional movement.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:42.031
The dot product adds products of matching components and produces a scalar

00:00:42.031 --> 00:00:48.560
Here the first contribution is two, the second is negative two, and the third is two

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.233
Including the hidden third coordinate changes the alignment calculation.

00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:59.709
Dividing this nonzero vector by its length preserves its direction and produces a unit vector

00:00:59.709 --> 00:01:05.885
Each component is scaled by one third, so each squared contribution scales by one ninth

00:01:05.885 --> 00:01:08.767
The new squared contributions add to one.
